{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0178FtSwPoYStoELHMLKmwRF","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Bootstrap Code Quality\n     2→\n     3→The constitution. Read this before adding code. Everything here is\n     4→load-bearing — every rule has been paid for at least once with a\n     5→session of debugging.\n     6→\n     7→## Directory layout\n     8→\n     9→```\n    10→bootstrap/src/\n    11→  main.fg                 ← entry point, argv handling, command dispatch\n    12→  core/                   ← stable infrastructure (ast, cursor, diagnostic, cg, resolver)\n    13→  parse/                  ← shared parser building blocks + statement dispatcher\n    14→  emit/                   ← shared codegen building blocks + statement dispatcher\n    15→  features/               ← one directory per language feature\n    16→    <name>/\n    17→      WHY.md              ← one paragraph\n    18→      grammar.md          ← EBNF fragment\n    19→      parser.fg           ← parses just this construct\n    20→      codegen.fg          ← emits IR for just this construct\n    21→      example.fg          ← canonical usage\n    22→      expected.out        ← expected stdout\n    23→```\n    24→\n    25→## What goes where\n    26→\n    27→> **A \"feature\" is anything keyword-introduced that is a top-level\n    28→> statement form.** Everything else is core or a phase building block.\n    29→\n    30→Features (each gets its own `features/<name>/` directory):\n    31→`if`, `while`, `for`, `match`, `let`, `mut`, `fn`, `struct`, `enum`,\n    32→`impl`, `extern`, `return`, `break`, `continue`.\n    33→\n    34→Not features (live in `parse/expr.fg`, `parse/pattern.fg`,\n    35→`parse/type_expr.fg`):\n    36→- Operators (`+`, `*`, `==`, etc) — they participate in precedence\n    37→- Literals (numbers, strings, bools, null)\n    38→- Postfix forms: calls `()`, indexing `[]`, field access `.x`\n    39→- Type annotations\n    40→- Patterns\n    41→\n    42→The line is bright: **does it have a keyword that introduces it as a\n    43→top-level statement?** If yes, feature. If no, building block.\n    44→\n    45→## Dependency rules\n    46→\n    47→The dependency arrow always points TOWARD core, never away from it.\n    48→\n    49→```\n    50→features/X/  →  parse/expr.fg, parse/pattern.fg, core/, etc.\n    51→              ↑\n    52→              never the reverse\n    53→```\n    54→\n    55→- A feature MAY import from `core/` and from any phase building block.\n    56→- A feature MAY NOT import from another feature.\n    57→- If two features need shared code, that code moves to `core/` or a\n    58→  phase building block.\n    59→- `core/` and phase building blocks may not import from `features/`.\n    60→  The dispatchers in `parse/stmt.fg` and `emit/stmt.fg` are the\n    61→  ONLY files that import from features, and they import only the\n    62→  per-feature parser/codegen entry points.\n    63→\n    64→## Hard rules (enforced by code review or pre-commit)\n    65→\n    66→These exist because each was paid for with at least one bug.\n    67→\n    68→### No fake successes\n    69→A function that can't perform the requested operation MUST return\n    70→null + an error, never a fake-success constant. This is the rule\n    71→that std-llvm now enforces. Project-wide.\n    72→\n    73→### No silent value loss in tail position\n    74→Empty `_ -> {}` arms in codegen are forbidden. Every Stmt and Expr\n    75→variant must be exhaustively handled in every emit/check function.\n    76→Until forge's exhaustive-match warning lands here, this is enforced\n    77→by code review and periodic grep.\n    78→\n    79→### No mutable globals\n    80→Every piece of compile state lives in the `Compiler` context struct\n    81→(or its sub-structs) and is threaded explicitly. No `mut CG_FOO` at\n    82→module scope. Globals are spooky action at a distance.\n    83→\n    84→### No circular module imports\n    85→Module dependencies must form a DAG. If `a` uses `b`, then `b` must\n    86→not transitively use `a`. Forge's host compiler may technically\n    87→allow cycles, but we forbid them as policy because:\n    88→\n    89→  - Go, Swift, Elm, OCaml all forbid them by language design\n    90→  - Cycles are usually a sign that two modules should be one, OR\n    91→    that there's a missing third module both should depend on\n    92→  - Cycles make refactoring fragile and reading order ambiguous\n    93→  - \"It works\" is not the same as \"it's correct\"\n    94→\n    95→When you need a cycle, **extract the shared concept into a\n    96→parent module that both sides depend on.** The dispatcher pattern\n    97→is the canonical example: if `codegen.fg` and `features/match/codegen.fg`\n    98→need to share types, those types live in `core/cg.fg` and both\n    99→sides depend on `core/cg.fg` one-way.\n   100→\n   101→This rule exists because the obvious-feeling shortcut during the\n   102→feature migration (Phase 4b) was to make codegen.fg and\n   103→features/match/codegen.fg circular. The correct answer was a\n   104→shared `core/cg.fg`. The principle costs an extra file but earns\n   105→a clean DAG forever.\n   106→\n   107→### One construct per file\n   108→A `features/<name>/parser.fg` parses ONE construct (or one tightly\n   109→related family — e.g. a `match` parser owns both the statement\n   110→form and the expression form because they share an arm-list and\n   111→pattern grammar). If a file starts handling unrelated constructs,\n   112→split it.\n   113→\n   114→There is **no hard line-count limit**. A file should be as long as\n   115→the one concept it owns naturally requires. Some constructs (a\n   116→fully-developed pattern matcher, the precedence climber for\n   117→expressions) are legitimately long. Others (a `return` parser) are\n   118→tiny. The rule is about *cohesion*, not size.\n   119→\n   120→The signal that a file is too big is not \"it has more than N\n   121→lines\" — it's \"I keep scrolling past unrelated code to find the\n   122→thing I want.\" When that happens, look for a missing concept and\n   123→extract it. When it doesn't, the file is fine at any length.\n   124→\n   125→### Forbidden vocabulary\n   126→These words are banned in identifiers and filenames:\n   127→`Manager`, `Helper`, `Util`, `Misc`, `Common`, `Handler`, `Service`,\n   128→`Wrapper`, `Shim`. They are dumping grounds. Use specific names.\n   129→\n   130→### File header conventions\n   131→Every `.fg` file starts with:\n   132→```\n   133→// WHY: <one sentence saying what this file owns>\n   134→```\n   135→\n   136→Optionally, a more detailed `// AGENT:` block telling future agents\n   137→when to read this file and where related concepts live.\n   138→\n   139→### `// INVARIANT:` on every mut\n   140→Every `mut` field in a struct and every mutable global gets an\n   141→`// INVARIANT: ...` comment saying what's always true. If you can't\n   142→write the invariant, you don't understand the state well enough to\n   143→mutate it.\n   144→\n   145→## Self-hosting invariants (defended by pre-commit)\n   146→\n   147→These have NAMES because invariants with names are defended:\n   148→\n   149→### Fixed-Point Invariant\n   150→bs2 and bs3 emit byte-identical IR for `bootstrap/src/main.fg`.\n   151→Enforced by `make selfhost` and the pre-commit hook.\n   152→\n   153→### Cross-Compiler Invariant\n   154→stage1 and bs2 produce identical stdout for every regression test.\n   155→Enforced by `make regress` and the pre-commit hook.\n   156→\n   157→### Score Invariant\n   158→`make score` reports zero wide-store-into-narrow-malloc bugs (the\n   159→heap-corruption guard). Enforced by `make score`.\n   160→\n   161→### Phase Isolation Invariant\n   162→No phase reaches into a previous phase's input. Parser doesn't\n   163→re-tokenize. Codegen doesn't re-parse. Enforced by code review.\n   164→\n   165→If a commit breaks any of these, **revert immediately** and\n   166→reconsider. We do not ship \"we'll fix it next commit\" workarounds.\n   167→\n   168→## Adding a feature\n   169→\n   170→Steps for adding a new language feature, in order:\n   171→\n   172→1. Create `bootstrap/src/features/<name>/`.\n   173→2. Write `WHY.md` (one paragraph) and `grammar.md` (EBNF fragment).\n   174→3. Write `parser.fg` with a `parse_<name>` function.\n   175→4. Add one line to `parse/stmt.fg`'s dispatcher importing and\n   176→   routing to `parse_<name>`.\n   177→5. Write `codegen.fg` with an `emit_<name>` function.\n   178→6. Add one line to `emit/stmt.fg`'s dispatcher likewise.\n   179→7. Write `example.fg` and `expected.out` (canonical usage).\n   180→8. Run `make test`. If it passes, commit.\n   181→9. Run `make selfhost`. If it passes, commit.\n   182→10. Verify the feature appears in the assembled `GRAMMAR.md` after\n   183→    `make grammar`.\n   184→\n   185→Removing a feature is `rm -r features/<name>/` plus removing the\n   186→two dispatcher lines plus a `make test`.\n   187→\n   188→## Anti-patterns (red flags in code review)\n   189→\n   190→- A file that mixes unrelated concepts (split it by concept, not by size)\n   191→- A file named `*_helpers.fg`, `*_utils.fg`, etc. (be specific)\n   192→- A `_ -> {}` match arm in codegen or check (silent value loss)\n   193→- A function returning a hardcoded constant on failure (fake success)\n   194→- A `mut` global without an `// INVARIANT:` comment\n   195→- An import edge from one feature to another (use core)\n   196→- A new keyword construct that isn't in `features/` (move it)\n   197→- A `// TODO:` in code (move to PLAN.md backlog)\n   198→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"99531a4f-114c-49ff-a929-dced21bdd83f","uuid":"a45d3b50-3777-4790-bee9-0e17317ddd84","timestamp":"2026-04-09T09:47:14.768Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/tristan/projects/tristanMatthias/forge-crafting-intepreters/bootstrap/CODE_QUALITY.md","content":"# Bootstrap Code Quality\n\nThe constitution. Read this before adding code. Everything here is\nload-bearing — every rule has been paid for at least once with a\nsession of debugging.\n\n## Directory layout\n\n```\nbootstrap/src/\n  main.fg                 ← entry point, argv handling, command dispatch\n  core/                   ← stable infrastructure (ast, cursor, diagnostic, cg, resolver)\n  parse/                  ← shared parser building blocks + statement dispatcher\n  emit/                   ← shared codegen building blocks + statement dispatcher\n  features/               ← one directory per language feature\n    <name>/\n      WHY.md              ← one paragraph\n      grammar.md          ← EBNF fragment\n      parser.fg           ← parses just this construct\n      codegen.fg          ← emits IR for just this construct\n      example.fg          ← canonical usage\n      expected.out        ← expected stdout\n```\n\n## What goes where\n\n> **A \"feature\" is anything keyword-introduced that is a top-level\n> statement form.** Everything else is core or a phase building block.\n\nFeatures (each gets its own `features/<name>/` directory):\n`if`, `while`, `for`, `match`, `let`, `mut`, `fn`, `struct`, `enum`,\n`impl`, `extern`, `return`, `break`, `continue`.\n\nNot features (live in `parse/expr.fg`, `parse/pattern.fg`,\n`parse/type_expr.fg`):\n- Operators (`+`, `*`, `==`, etc) — they participate in precedence\n- Literals (numbers, strings, bools, null)\n- Postfix forms: calls `()`, indexing `[]`, field access `.x`\n- Type annotations\n- Patterns\n\nThe line is bright: **does it have a keyword that introduces it as a\ntop-level statement?** If yes, feature. If no, building block.\n\n## Dependency rules\n\nThe dependency arrow always points TOWARD core, never away from it.\n\n```\nfeatures/X/  →  parse/expr.fg, parse/pattern.fg, core/, etc.\n              ↑\n              never the reverse\n```\n\n- A feature MAY import from `core/` and from any phase building block.\n- A feature MAY NOT import from another feature.\n- If two features need shared code, that code moves to `core/` or a\n  phase building block.\n- `core/` and phase building blocks may not import from `features/`.\n  The dispatchers in `parse/stmt.fg` and `emit/stmt.fg` are the\n  ONLY files that import from features, and they import only the\n  per-feature parser/codegen entry points.\n\n## Hard rules (enforced by code review or pre-commit)\n\nThese exist because each was paid for with at least one bug.\n\n### No fake successes\nA function that can't perform the requested operation MUST return\nnull + an error, never a fake-success constant. This is the rule\nthat std-llvm now enforces. Project-wide.\n\n### No silent value loss in tail position\nEmpty `_ -> {}` arms in codegen are forbidden. Every Stmt and Expr\nvariant must be exhaustively handled in every emit/check function.\nUntil forge's exhaustive-match warning lands here, this is enforced\nby code review and periodic grep.\n\n### No mutable globals\nEvery piece of compile state lives in the `Compiler` context struct\n(or its sub-structs) and is threaded explicitly. No `mut CG_FOO` at\nmodule scope. Globals are spooky action at a distance.\n\n### No circular module imports\nModule dependencies must form a DAG. If `a` uses `b`, then `b` must\nnot transitively use `a`. Forge's host compiler may technically\nallow cycles, but we forbid them as policy because:\n\n  - Go, Swift, Elm, OCaml all forbid them by language design\n  - Cycles are usually a sign that two modules should be one, OR\n    that there's a missing third module both should depend on\n  - Cycles make refactoring fragile and reading order ambiguous\n  - \"It works\" is not the same as \"it's correct\"\n\nWhen you need a cycle, **extract the shared concept into a\nparent module that both sides depend on.** The dispatcher pattern\nis the canonical example: if `codegen.fg` and `features/match/codegen.fg`\nneed to share types, those types live in `core/cg.fg` and both\nsides depend on `core/cg.fg` one-way.\n\nThis rule exists because the obvious-feeling shortcut during the\nfeature migration (Phase 4b) was to make codegen.fg and\nfeatures/match/codegen.fg circular. The correct answer was a\nshared `core/cg.fg`. The principle costs an extra file but earns\na clean DAG forever.\n\n### One construct per file\nA `features/<name>/parser.fg` parses ONE construct (or one tightly\nrelated family — e.g. a `match` parser owns both the statement\nform and the expression form because they share an arm-list and\npattern grammar). If a file starts handling unrelated constructs,\nsplit it.\n\nThere is **no hard line-count limit**. A file should be as long as\nthe one concept it owns naturally requires. Some constructs (a\nfully-developed pattern matcher, the precedence climber for\nexpressions) are legitimately long. Others (a `return` parser) are\ntiny. The rule is about *cohesion*, not size.\n\nThe signal that a file is too big is not \"it has more than N\nlines\" — it's \"I keep scrolling past unrelated code to find the\nthing I want.\" When that happens, look for a missing concept and\nextract it. When it doesn't, the file is fine at any length.\n\n### Forbidden vocabulary\nThese words are banned in identifiers and filenames:\n`Manager`, `Helper`, `Util`, `Misc`, `Common`, `Handler`, `Service`,\n`Wrapper`, `Shim`. They are dumping grounds. Use specific names.\n\n### File header conventions\nEvery `.fg` file starts with:\n```\n// WHY: <one sentence saying what this file owns>\n```\n\nOptionally, a more detailed `// AGENT:` block telling future agents\nwhen to read this file and where related concepts live.\n\n### `// INVARIANT:` on every mut\nEvery `mut` field in a struct and every mutable global gets an\n`// INVARIANT: ...` comment saying what's always true. If you can't\nwrite the invariant, you don't understand the state well enough to\nmutate it.\n\n## Self-hosting invariants (defended by pre-commit)\n\nThese have NAMES because invariants with names are defended:\n\n### Fixed-Point Invariant\nbs2 and bs3 emit byte-identical IR for `bootstrap/src/main.fg`.\nEnforced by `make selfhost` and the pre-commit hook.\n\n### Cross-Compiler Invariant\nstage1 and bs2 produce identical stdout for every regression test.\nEnforced by `make regress` and the pre-commit hook.\n\n### Score Invariant\n`make score` reports zero wide-store-into-narrow-malloc bugs (the\nheap-corruption guard). Enforced by `make score`.\n\n### Phase Isolation Invariant\nNo phase reaches into a previous phase's input. Parser doesn't\nre-tokenize. Codegen doesn't re-parse. Enforced by code review.\n\nIf a commit breaks any of these, **revert immediately** and\nreconsider. We do not ship \"we'll fix it next commit\" workarounds.\n\n## Adding a feature\n\nSteps for adding a new language feature, in order:\n\n1. Create `bootstrap/src/features/<name>/`.\n2. Write `WHY.md` (one paragraph) and `grammar.md` (EBNF fragment).\n3. Write `parser.fg` with a `parse_<name>` function.\n4. Add one line to `parse/stmt.fg`'s dispatcher importing and\n   routing to `parse_<name>`.\n5. Write `codegen.fg` with an `emit_<name>` function.\n6. Add one line to `emit/stmt.fg`'s dispatcher likewise.\n7. Write `example.fg` and `expected.out` (canonical usage).\n8. Run `make test`. If it passes, commit.\n9. Run `make selfhost`. If it passes, commit.\n10. Verify the feature appears in the assembled `GRAMMAR.md` after\n    `make grammar`.\n\nRemoving a feature is `rm -r features/<name>/` plus removing the\ntwo dispatcher lines plus a `make test`.\n\n## Anti-patterns (red flags in code review)\n\n- A file that mixes unrelated concepts (split it by concept, not by size)\n- A file named `*_helpers.fg`, `*_utils.fg`, etc. (be specific)\n- A `_ -> {}` match arm in codegen or check (silent value loss)\n- A function returning a hardcoded constant on failure (fake success)\n- A `mut` global without an `// INVARIANT:` comment\n- An import edge from one feature to another (use core)\n- A new keyword construct that isn't in `features/` (move it)\n- A `// TODO:` in code (move to PLAN.md backlog)\n","numLines":198,"startLine":1,"totalLines":198}}}
